  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">Starting the site</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    To have a run start the site itself, give
    <code>site.serve</code> the command that starts it. Without it, shotlist expects something to already
    be answering at
    <code>site.url</code>.
  </p>
  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={serve} file="shotlist.config.yaml" />
  </div>

  <p class="mt-6 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    <span class="font-medium text-ink">A server already up is used as it is.</span> shotlist fetches
    <code>site.url</code> first and starts nothing when something answers, so this can stay in the config
    while you write recipes with the dev server open in another terminal. CI, where nothing is listening,
    is where it actually launches one.
  </p>

  <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    What a run starts, it stops — including on Ctrl-C. The command runs in its own process group,
    because <code>npm run dev</code> is npm, which spawns the server: signalling only the process shotlist
    launched would leave the one holding the port.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={serveLong} file="shotlist.config.yaml" />
  </div>

  <DocTable
    head={['Key', 'Default', 'What it sets']}
    rows={[
      ['<code>command</code>', 'required', 'The program to run, and its arguments'],
      [
        '<code>ready</code>',
        '<code>site.url</code>',
        'What proves it is up: a URL, a port, or <code>{ log: &lt;pattern&gt; }</code>',
      ],
      [
        '<code>cwd</code>',
        "the config's directory",
        'Where to run it, resolved from the config file',
      ],
      ['<code>env</code>', '<code>{}</code>', "Environment for the command, on top of the run's"],
      ['<code>timeout</code>', '<code>30000</code>', 'How long to wait for it to answer'],
    ]}
  />

  <p class="mt-6 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    <code>ready</code> is what proves the server is up, and defaults to fetching <code
      >site.url</code
    >. A URL is ready on any response at all, including a 404: that something answered is the
    question, not what it said. A port is ready when it accepts a connection, and
    <code>{'{ log: … }'}</code> when the pattern matches the server's output.
  </p>

  <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    <span class="font-medium text-ink">There is no shell.</span> The command is run directly, so
    <code>&amp;&amp;</code>,
    <code>|</code>, <code> &gt;</code> and a <code>VAR=value</code> prefix are refused rather than half-understood
    — environment goes under <code>env:</code>, and anything needing a shell goes in a script you
    run instead. A run of only
    <code>source: file</code> recipes never opens the site, and does not start one.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pages-that-need-signing-in" class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">
    Pages that need signing in
  </h2>
  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    To shoot a page behind an account, name a session under
    <code>site.sessions</code> and point a recipe at it with
    <code>session:</code>. A session is the state a signed-in browser carries — its cookies and
    local storage — written by
    <a href="/docs/running-shotlist#signing-in"><code>shotlist --login</code></a> and read back before
    the shot.
  </p>
  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={sessions} file="shotlist.config.yaml" />
  </div>
  <p class="mt-6 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    <span class="font-medium text-ink">You do not create the JSON file, and never edit it.</span> The path
    says where <code>shotlist --login admin</code> should write, and that command creates the directory
    as well. What it writes is what the browser had after you signed in — a JSON of cookies and per-origin
    local storage — which is Playwright's format and not something to hand-write. A recipe asking for
    a session that has not been written yet stops the run and names the command that writes it.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    A bare path is the whole of it; the mapping adds
    <code>verify</code>. They are named rather than singular because the same page is often worth
    shooting twice — once as an administrator, once as somebody with no rights at all — and the two
    shots differ only in which session opened them.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    <span class="font-medium text-ink">
      <code class="font-mono">verify</code> is a selector only a signed-in page has</span
    > — an avatar, an admin bar, a sign-out link. It is worth setting, because a session that has expired
    does not fail: it redirects to the sign-in form, and without something to check for, every shot of
    the run is a picture of that form and <code>--install</code> commits them. With it, the run stops
    and names the command that signs in again.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    <span class="font-medium text-ink"
      >The file holds live cookies. Keep it out of version control</span
    > — anyone with it is signed in as whoever made it. What is <em>not</em> in it, or anywhere else shotlist
    reads, is the password: that was typed into a browser, or came from a variable the operator allowed.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">Image format</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    <code>png</code> keeps every pixel and is the default.
    <code>webp</code> is the one worth reaching for: a shot of this site's homepage is 87 KB as a PNG
    and 33 KB as a WebP.
    <code>jpeg</code> is lossy in the way that shows worst on what a screenshot of an interface is mostly
    made of, which is text.
  </p>
  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={imageFormat} file="shotlist.config.yaml" />
  </div>
  <p class="mt-6 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    A recipe may set its own <code>format</code> and
    <code>quality</code>.
    <code>quality</code> runs from
    <code>1</code> to
    <code>100</code> and does nothing to a PNG, which has no detail to trade away. The file is named for
    the format —
    <code>.png</code>,
    <code>.webp</code>, and
    <code>.jpg</code> for
    <code>jpeg</code> — and
    <code>--check</code> compares against a committed file of the same name.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    <span class="font-medium text-ink">There is no AVIF.</span> The only encoder here is the browser doing
    the drawing, and Chromium reads AVIF but will not write it — it answers a request for one with a PNG
    rather than an error, so every conversion checks what it got back.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">Style</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    Every drawing constant is configurable, and a recipe may override any of them under its own
    <code>style:</code> key. This block is the reference for
    <code>style</code> — it is written out in full rather than tabulated, so the shape and the default
    sit together. Sizes are in image pixels, so they do not change when <code>scale</code> does. These
    are the defaults.
  </p>
  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={style} file="shotlist.config.yaml" />
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">Fonts</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    Set the label typeface under <code>style.label.font</code>. It has to be resolvable by the
    browser doing the drawing, which by default means installed on the machine, and a family that is
    not installed <span class="font-medium text-ink">falls back silently</span> — every label renders
    in another typeface with nothing to say so. shotlist measures the rendered text to catch that and
    warns. It is a warning rather than an error: a fallback still produces an image, and which faces a
    machine has is not something a recipe can know.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    To use a font that is not installed, give <code>fontUrl</code> a stylesheet. A http(s) or <code
      >data:</code
    > URL is fetched when the callouts are drawn, so that one needs network access at shoot time.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    <span class="font-medium text-ink">Anything else is a path</span>, resolved from the config
    file's directory the way every other path is — for a font the project ships, including one that
    was never online. It is read from disk and inlined, along with the font files it points at,
    because the page the callouts are drawn in is built in memory, and a browser gives such a page
    no <code>file:</code> subresource. An absolute
    <code>file:</code> URL works too.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    <span class="font-medium text-ink">The path may be the font file itself</span> — a
    <code>.woff2</code>,
    <code>.woff</code>,
    <code>.otf</code> or
    <code>.ttf</code>. shotlist declares it for you, under the first real family in <code
      >style.label.font</code
    > and at that label's weight, so a stack of nothing but
    <code>sans-serif</code> and its like is refused: there is no name to declare it under. One file is
    one face. A project needing several — a regular and a bold — writes the stylesheet and points at that
    instead.
  </p>
  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={fontFile} file="shotlist.config.yaml" />
  </div>
  <p class="mt-6 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    A font that has to be fetched is waited for, and the wait is bounded by
    <code>site.timeout</code>. Past that the labels are drawn in whatever the browser had, and the
    run says so — the same warning as a family that was never there. A run is delayed by a slow font
    server, never held open by one.
  </p>
  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={font} file="shotlist.config.yaml" />
  </div>
  <div class="mt-4">
    <RecipeCard code={localFont} file="shotlist.config.yaml" />
  </div>
  <p class="mt-6 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    <code>strokeWidth</code> is centred on the glyph outline, so half of it is painted under the fill.
    A 6-pixel stroke reads as a 3-pixel outline; tools that draw the stroke entirely outside need roughly
    double the number here to match.
  </p>
</Docs>
